1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
They are technological wonders that have raced their way to the top of the acceleration curve,

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:10,000
breaking records and leaving the competition in the dust.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:12,000
They are the swiftest of the swift.

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:17,000
Now, the world's fastest on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:43,980
On this muggy day in Nardo, Italy, a Challenger from rural Sweden is preparing to try to break the record of world's fastest production car.

6
00:00:44,260 --> 00:00:53,060
Set by the McLaren F1, this record has defied all takers for seven years and stands at a blistering 240.3 miles per hour.

7
00:00:54,160 --> 00:00:59,220
A humid day and a tightly ovaled track seem to pretend another failure.

8
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,040
Nardo is a circular track so it kind of scrubs away a lot of speed you're

9
00:01:05,041 --> 00:01:12,520
constantly on a turn. But the Challenger is no ordinary car. This is the CCR from

10
00:01:12,521 --> 00:01:16,560
the innovative Koenigsegg motor company.

11
00:01:17,060 --> 00:01:24,560
It quickly accelerates past 100 miles per hour and then 150. By the time the CCR

12
00:01:24,561 --> 00:01:29,880
is in sixth gear it's well over 200 miles per hour rapidly closing in on the

13
00:01:29,881 --> 00:01:30,847
old record.

14
00:01:30,848 --> 00:01:37,880
The man behind the new challenger, Christian von Konnicksegg, had an infatuation

15
00:01:37,881 --> 00:01:40,040
with fast cars that began in his youth.

16
00:01:40,041 --> 00:01:46,580
Christian von Konnicksegg When I was five years old, I saw a Norwegian puppet movie

17
00:01:46,581 --> 00:01:50,651
about a bicycle repairman who built himself a race car.

18
00:01:50,652 --> 00:01:53,020
And I got really, really excited

19
00:01:53,021 --> 00:01:59,120
by it. So I said to myself, that's what I want to do. I want to build my own car.

20
00:01:59,121 --> 00:02:05,240
That dream led Christian to start the Koenigsegg Automotive Company in 1995.

21
00:02:05,241 --> 00:02:09,820
Soon thereafter, he designed the CCR.

22
00:02:09,821 --> 00:02:15,100
I basically started from a blank sheet of paper and I had some ideas of what my car

23
00:02:15,101 --> 00:02:16,100
would be like.

24
00:02:16,101 --> 00:02:20,780
It was going to be a nimble, lightweight, high-performance sports car with a detachable

25
00:02:20,781 --> 00:02:25,460
hardtop that actually gave the convertible feeling when you take it off.

26
00:02:25,461 --> 00:02:29,300
The CCR stands for Competition Coupe Radical.

27
00:02:29,301 --> 00:02:35,460
The Koenigsegg CCR is the lightest, most powerful, fastest supercar ever built.

28
00:02:35,461 --> 00:02:42,540
It's basically a race car for the road, but it has features that you expect from a road

29
00:02:42,541 --> 00:02:49,780
car such as air conditioning, stereo, power windows, power steering, etc.

30
00:02:49,781 --> 00:02:56,780
It's got racing car performance, but it's still very streetable and drivable and you

31
00:02:56,781 --> 00:03:00,820
can cruise around in it.

32
00:03:00,821 --> 00:03:03,780
Our production facility used to be a fighter jet squadron.

33
00:03:03,781 --> 00:03:10,780
Now we build the fastest car in the world in here.

34
00:03:10,781 --> 00:03:15,780
Under that car's sleek hood is an engine that holds a world's record for sheer power.

35
00:03:15,781 --> 00:03:17,551
This is the engine for the CCR.

36
00:03:17,552 --> 00:03:21,780
It's the most powerful production engine in the world with 806 horsepower.

37
00:03:21,781 --> 00:03:27,780
The CCR features a very lightweight V8 engine with a patented supercharging system.

38
00:03:27,781 --> 00:03:30,780
We base it off a production racing block.

39
00:03:36,780 --> 00:03:39,780
All the internals are our own engineering.

40
00:03:39,781 --> 00:03:50,020
We were looking at doing V12, but we quickly realized that our performance target of 800 horsepower could be obtained safely within a V8 structure.

41
00:03:53,360 --> 00:03:57,480
One of the CCR's most striking features is its unique door mechanism.

42
00:03:58,920 --> 00:04:06,040
We have a very wide car with very wide doors, so we realized that when we parked next to other cars, that was not very practical.

43
00:04:06,041 --> 00:04:15,080
And I had seen on an electrical car in Sweden a similar door system where you open first outwards and then upwards, which was quite intriguing.

44
00:04:15,360 --> 00:04:18,860
But it wasn't really practical to first move out and then up.

45
00:04:19,160 --> 00:04:24,960
So we came up with this system where we do it all in one motion, which according to everyone we talked to was impossible.

46
00:04:26,060 --> 00:04:31,140
It's probably the most difficult door system on any car in the history of cars.

47
00:04:31,420 --> 00:04:34,320
But it works very, very good and it's very, very stable.

48
00:04:36,040 --> 00:04:39,920
Another feature crucial to the car's success is much less obvious.

49
00:04:41,180 --> 00:04:44,540
There's precious little metal used in construction of the CCR.

50
00:04:45,940 --> 00:04:50,280
Instead, super high-tech carbon fiber makes up much of the car.

51
00:04:51,620 --> 00:04:55,865
The carbon fiber technology was originally invented by NASA in the U.S.

52
00:04:55,866 --> 00:04:57,300
for their space program.

53
00:04:57,680 --> 00:05:03,680
Basically, it's the strongest, lightest commercially available material to produce products out of.

54
00:05:03,681 --> 00:05:05,680
And it's still very, very expensive.

55
00:05:06,040 --> 00:05:09,820
But if you want to go lightweight and strong, carbon fiber is the way to go.

56
00:05:10,480 --> 00:05:16,220
In its original state, carbon fiber looks like the thin neoprene rubber used in wetsuits.

57
00:05:17,240 --> 00:05:21,200
The fiber is formed onto molds of the specific car part it's to become.

58
00:05:22,600 --> 00:05:28,640
Then on those molds, the parts are placed into a kiln-like oven, whose temperature is constantly monitored.

59
00:05:31,980 --> 00:05:34,600
This is the oven we use to cure our products in.

60
00:05:34,601 --> 00:05:37,440
We cure them for 120 degrees for one hour.

61
00:05:37,820 --> 00:05:40,180
Then it will be several more times stiffer than steel.

62
00:05:41,340 --> 00:05:44,800
The now super-hardened fiber remains remarkably lightweight.

63
00:05:45,960 --> 00:05:50,200
The molded pieces are then fitted together by hand.

64
00:05:50,760 --> 00:05:53,880
We use carbon fiber almost everywhere in the car,

65
00:05:54,040 --> 00:05:57,100
so that means the chassis structure is actually carbon fiber.

66
00:05:57,600 --> 00:06:01,000
The engine structures, even valve covers are carbon fiber.

67
00:06:01,001 --> 00:06:05,380
All the interior trim panels are very, very thin carbon fiber.

68
00:06:05,500 --> 00:06:07,860
Even the body panel system is carbon fiber.

69
00:06:08,120 --> 00:06:12,200
When you see them painted, they just look like steel or aluminum, but it's carbon fiber underneath.

70
00:06:15,520 --> 00:06:21,720
Technology this sophisticated wasn't even imaginable when automobiles made their first appearance in 1886.

71
00:06:22,720 --> 00:06:27,860
Built by Carl Benz, the first production car had three wheels and one cylinder.

72
00:06:28,760 --> 00:06:31,740
It'll do about 12 miles an hour, 13 miles an hour,

73
00:06:31,900 --> 00:06:36,120
but a horse would have to really be pushing it to go 14 miles in an hour,

74
00:06:36,320 --> 00:06:39,300
and so it was quite a bit, and he built 10 of them.

75
00:06:39,540 --> 00:06:41,280
I wasn't there, but I guarantee you,

76
00:06:41,520 --> 00:06:44,100
two of those cars went off against each other in a race.

77
00:06:44,820 --> 00:06:46,260
By the turn of the century,

78
00:06:46,700 --> 00:06:50,520
automobile speeds had increased enough to spawn the first sanctioned car races,

79
00:06:51,860 --> 00:06:53,840
including one in Grosse Pointe, Michigan,

80
00:06:54,440 --> 00:06:56,000
that carried historic implications.

81
00:06:56,001 --> 00:06:59,840
The Ford against the Wynton, and this is back in 1903,

82
00:06:59,841 --> 00:07:03,000
and there was a prize of $10,000.

83
00:07:03,001 --> 00:07:05,640
Now, Ford had gone bankrupt a couple of times,

84
00:07:05,641 --> 00:07:08,240
but he built this car for this race.

85
00:07:08,241 --> 00:07:11,040
The Wynton had mechanical trouble on the ninth lap

86
00:07:11,041 --> 00:07:13,480
and did not finish, and here comes Ford,

87
00:07:13,481 --> 00:07:14,960
goes on by him and wins the race,

88
00:07:14,961 --> 00:07:17,360
and that was the start of the Ford Motor Company.

89
00:07:17,361 --> 00:07:19,920
$10,000 in 1903 was a lot of money,

90
00:07:19,921 --> 00:07:22,200
and he took that money and he started building

91
00:07:22,201 --> 00:07:24,800
his first Model Ts, and the rest is history.

92
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:30,720
Ford's Model T came off the production line with a top speed of 45 miles per hour,

93
00:07:31,120 --> 00:07:36,300
an increase of nearly 400% over Benz's first models just 20 years earlier.

94
00:07:38,660 --> 00:07:42,160
But Ford soon had serious competition in the speed department.

95
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:48,440
Among the fastest production cars of the teens was the aptly named Raceabout,

96
00:07:48,920 --> 00:07:51,720
built by the Mercer Automobile Company in Hamilton, New Jersey.

97
00:07:51,721 --> 00:07:57,920
There were two-seater cars, what today would be considered a sports car,

98
00:07:58,020 --> 00:08:00,320
although you sat on the body, not in the body,

99
00:08:00,720 --> 00:08:05,140
and they were considered stylish, fast automobiles.

100
00:08:05,440 --> 00:08:07,400
We have a 1912 Mercer in our collection,

101
00:08:07,600 --> 00:08:10,100
and that car will do an honest 95 miles an hour.

102
00:08:10,260 --> 00:08:12,240
We're talking almost 100 years ago.

103
00:08:12,241 --> 00:08:14,740
That was supersonic for its age.

104
00:08:15,080 --> 00:08:17,240
But it wouldn't be until the early 1930s

105
00:08:17,900 --> 00:08:21,020
that a production car would smash through 100 miles per hour.

106
00:08:21,720 --> 00:08:24,220
the Duesenberg.

107
00:08:24,221 --> 00:08:26,560
Duesenberg was actually a race car builder,

108
00:08:26,561 --> 00:08:29,320
and he raced in Indianapolis.

109
00:08:29,321 --> 00:08:31,400
Duesenbergs, they would go in second gear,

110
00:08:31,401 --> 00:08:33,400
they would do 90 miles an hour.

111
00:08:33,401 --> 00:08:36,600
And top gear, they were good for maybe 105 miles an hour.

112
00:08:36,601 --> 00:08:38,740
Now, they only made a little over 500 Duesenbergs

113
00:08:38,741 --> 00:08:41,580
altogether between 1930 and 1936,

114
00:08:41,581 --> 00:08:47,120
but that was considered a very, very fast production car.

115
00:08:47,121 --> 00:08:48,940
The decade before World War II

116
00:08:48,941 --> 00:08:50,840
saw technical advancements,

117
00:08:50,841 --> 00:08:53,140
like Buick's Dynaflash straight-8 engine.

118
00:08:53,800 --> 00:08:56,880
These powerhouses helped keep the speed crown in the U.S.,

119
00:08:56,881 --> 00:08:58,600
with cars like the Buick Century.

120
00:09:01,640 --> 00:09:05,860
But after the war, European automakers made a determined run at the title,

121
00:09:06,360 --> 00:09:09,740
starting with a small but powerful two-seater from the U.K.

122
00:09:09,741 --> 00:09:11,480
that pushed the mark even higher.

123
00:09:13,340 --> 00:09:16,120
They decided to designate the car, the XK120,

124
00:09:16,400 --> 00:09:18,440
based on the fact that it would go 120 miles an hour.

125
00:09:18,760 --> 00:09:20,080
And Jaguar's done that through the years.

126
00:09:20,840 --> 00:09:24,920
The XK120 and the ensuing E-Class of Jaguar roadsters

127
00:09:24,921 --> 00:09:28,740
changed the world's perception of what a fast streetcar looked like.

128
00:09:31,380 --> 00:09:34,500
Soon, other European designers like Enzo Ferrari

129
00:09:34,501 --> 00:09:37,480
began production models based on racing success.

130
00:09:40,580 --> 00:09:43,180
Ferrari's full emphasis for himself was racing.

131
00:09:43,420 --> 00:09:47,000
But to finance race cars, he built production automobiles.

132
00:09:47,100 --> 00:09:49,280
We're talking cars that you and I could get in,

133
00:09:49,281 --> 00:09:52,880
hit the key, start up, turn the radio on if you want to listen to this music.

134
00:09:53,800 --> 00:09:57,780
By the late 1960s, other European builders upped the ante,

135
00:09:58,400 --> 00:10:00,500
attacking the 200-mile-an-hour barrier,

136
00:10:00,780 --> 00:10:03,520
like one Perruzzo Lamborghini.

137
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:08,440
Lamborghini was a farm implement manufacturer,

138
00:10:08,740 --> 00:10:09,880
and he was a very wealthy man.

139
00:10:10,260 --> 00:10:11,900
So he went back to his plant, and he said,

140
00:10:11,980 --> 00:10:13,300
all right, we're going to build a sports car,

141
00:10:13,460 --> 00:10:15,480
and we're going to go head-to-head with Ferrari,

142
00:10:15,580 --> 00:10:16,760
and he did the Contas.

143
00:10:17,340 --> 00:10:21,580
The Contosh was an absolute knock-your-socks-off,

144
00:10:21,720 --> 00:10:23,500
you know, just can't believe it,

145
00:10:23,600 --> 00:10:25,160
you know, take-your-breath-away type of car.

146
00:10:25,400 --> 00:10:26,340
It was so revolutionary.

147
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:28,620
And they claimed they were the first production car

148
00:10:28,621 --> 00:10:29,760
to go 200 miles an hour.

149
00:10:30,800 --> 00:10:33,600
Soon other cars were challenging even higher speeds.

150
00:10:34,620 --> 00:10:37,800
As before, most had their origins on the track.

151
00:10:38,800 --> 00:10:40,880
McLaren is a race car company.

152
00:10:41,320 --> 00:10:43,920
Bruce McLaren was a race car driver, an Australian.

153
00:10:43,921 --> 00:10:47,380
He came to Europe and was driving for other people

154
00:10:47,381 --> 00:10:49,600
and then decided to design and build his own cars.

155
00:10:49,760 --> 00:10:51,860
And they thought that it would be a great idea

156
00:10:51,861 --> 00:10:53,340
with all this technology they had,

157
00:10:53,420 --> 00:10:54,600
particularly from Formula One,

158
00:10:54,720 --> 00:10:56,540
to build a car for the street.

159
00:10:57,480 --> 00:11:00,460
The McLaren F1 became one of the most celebrated

160
00:11:00,461 --> 00:11:02,080
production cars ever created.

161
00:11:04,140 --> 00:11:06,600
Featuring a unique driver's center cockpit,

162
00:11:07,220 --> 00:11:09,780
it had a powerful BMW V12 engine

163
00:11:09,781 --> 00:11:13,080
that cranked out 627 horsepower.

164
00:11:13,920 --> 00:11:22,920
Introduced in 1991, the F1 went on to set the world production speed record, just over

165
00:11:22,921 --> 00:11:30,240
240 miles per hour on March 31st, 1998, only two months before the final F1 rolled out

166
00:11:30,241 --> 00:11:37,420
of the factory.

167
00:11:37,421 --> 00:11:43,340
But on that muggy Italian day in February 2005, after seven years, that mark was about

168
00:11:43,341 --> 00:11:45,460
to become history.

169
00:11:45,461 --> 00:11:49,840
The Koenigsegg CCR ran smooth and hot.

170
00:11:49,841 --> 00:11:53,280
It powered up to 240 and then smashed through

171
00:11:53,281 --> 00:11:58,260
with a sizzling, record-breaking 241 miles per hour,

172
00:11:58,261 --> 00:12:01,260
making it the new world's fastest production car.

173
00:12:04,920 --> 00:12:08,220
Of course, because the CCR is a production model,

174
00:12:08,221 --> 00:12:10,280
anyone can place an order.

175
00:12:10,281 --> 00:12:13,180
But you'd better start saving now.

176
00:12:13,181 --> 00:12:20,200
The basic price in US dollars with the current exchange rate would be around 590,000 US dollars, so it's quite high.

177
00:12:21,960 --> 00:12:25,880
But for that price, you get to drive a technological wonder.

178
00:12:26,620 --> 00:12:31,200
The world record-holding CCR, fondly referred to as the Ghost.

179
00:12:31,880 --> 00:12:34,840
We have a special symbol for the CCR. It's a ghost.

180
00:12:35,380 --> 00:12:38,540
The ghost version symbolizes the fastest possible Koenigsegg.

181
00:12:38,541 --> 00:12:40,640
We have a lot of models within Koenigsegg,

182
00:12:40,641 --> 00:12:44,080
and one that I like the most is, if you haven't seen the future,

183
00:12:44,200 --> 00:12:45,460
you haven't driven fast enough.

184
00:12:47,880 --> 00:12:49,860
But perhaps you'll glimpse the future

185
00:12:49,861 --> 00:12:53,860
from a world's fastest machine that literally floats on air.

186
00:12:56,380 --> 00:12:59,400
The CCR's specially formulated Michelin tires

187
00:12:59,401 --> 00:13:02,940
are rated for speeds up to 250 miles per hour.

188
00:13:08,540 --> 00:13:17,040
In Shanghai, China, it's opening day for a train line unlike any other on Earth.

189
00:13:17,041 --> 00:13:21,460
Almost silently, the streamlined coaches begin their journey.

190
00:13:21,461 --> 00:13:35,461
Soon, they're traveling at a very untrain-like speed, a blistering 270 miles per hour.

191
00:13:36,180 --> 00:13:39,420
We actually like to think of our brain as an airplane without wheels.

192
00:13:39,421 --> 00:13:41,920
We're flying on the ground, so to speak.

193
00:13:41,921 --> 00:13:46,320
A remarkable propulsion system has started a revolution in rail travel.

194
00:13:46,321 --> 00:13:49,900
Magnetic levitation, or simply, maglev.

195
00:13:49,901 --> 00:13:57,480
Magnets, when opposite poles are put together, will tend to propel themselves away from each

196
00:13:57,481 --> 00:13:58,480
other.

197
00:13:58,481 --> 00:14:03,620
Now, take that technology and apply it in a controlled manner to a rail vehicle, and there's

198
00:14:03,621 --> 00:14:05,920
incredible speeds that can be attained.

199
00:14:10,200 --> 00:14:15,340
The Shanghai Maglev system was developed by the Berlin-based company TransRapid.

200
00:14:17,220 --> 00:14:23,360
Here, in rural Emsland, Germany, TransRapid's test facility features a 12-mile track

201
00:14:23,361 --> 00:14:28,620
that treats paying passengers with speeds nearing 275 miles per hour.

202
00:14:30,980 --> 00:14:33,080
Maglev actually goes back a long ways.

203
00:14:33,081 --> 00:14:36,340
The first patents were produced in the mid-30s,

204
00:14:36,420 --> 00:14:41,620
but it took a long time before the rest of the technology actually caught up with it.

205
00:14:42,480 --> 00:14:46,580
Transrapid began the R&D on magnetic levitation in the late 60s.

206
00:14:47,300 --> 00:14:51,060
And during the 1970s, we went through a series of test vehicles

207
00:14:51,061 --> 00:14:53,300
to prove the concept, prove the technology.

208
00:14:54,620 --> 00:15:00,500
The technology behind magnetic levitation is both remarkably high-tech and deceptively simple.

209
00:15:03,080 --> 00:15:14,560
Maglev works by taking electromagnets and using them to lift a train up from underneath and guide it down a track.

210
00:15:14,561 --> 00:15:17,980
The train itself rides on an electromagnetic field.

211
00:15:18,720 --> 00:15:21,480
To support the train we have what we call support magnets.

212
00:15:22,020 --> 00:15:27,620
We have an iron core and then we have aluminum ribbon type coils wrapped around it.

213
00:15:27,621 --> 00:15:33,020
And then also integrated into that is what we call a linear generator, which is a special

214
00:15:33,021 --> 00:15:38,180
piece of equipment we use to transfer energy from the guideway up into the train as it's

215
00:15:38,181 --> 00:15:40,180
going along.

216
00:15:40,181 --> 00:15:45,180
Each of the magnets also has an epoxy case around it, which makes it impervious to the

217
00:15:45,181 --> 00:15:46,180
environment.

218
00:15:46,181 --> 00:15:50,620
The support magnets are clustered together underneath the train cars.

219
00:15:50,621 --> 00:15:55,220
This is a Trent Rapid electromagnet for levitation.

220
00:15:55,221 --> 00:15:57,600
It has 12 coils.

221
00:15:57,601 --> 00:16:02,220
Each train section has about 16 levitation magnets that lift the vehicle up.

222
00:16:02,221 --> 00:16:06,480
A train with three sections has 560 of these coils.

223
00:16:06,481 --> 00:16:09,720
There's about half that number that are guiding the train.

224
00:16:09,721 --> 00:16:12,900
They're located on the right and left sides of the train and they guide it as it moves

225
00:16:12,901 --> 00:16:13,900
down the track.

226
00:16:13,901 --> 00:16:19,940
Thus, maglev trains have no use for rails or even wheels.

227
00:16:19,941 --> 00:16:23,720
There is also no true locomotive.

228
00:16:23,721 --> 00:16:28,220
In contrast to traditional trains, our motor is in the guideway itself.

229
00:16:28,221 --> 00:16:32,360
The train levitates, but otherwise it's passive.

230
00:16:32,361 --> 00:16:36,960
Inside the guideway is what is termed a long stator linear motor.

231
00:16:36,961 --> 00:16:42,880
Similar in principle to a conventional rotating electric motor, here the stator, or windings,

232
00:16:42,881 --> 00:16:47,480
have been laid flat along the underside of the guideway.

233
00:16:47,481 --> 00:16:52,100
Instead of a conventional motor's rotating electromagnetic wave, a traveling wave is sent

234
00:16:52,101 --> 00:16:54,700
down the length of the windings.

235
00:16:54,701 --> 00:16:57,140
This attracts the support magnets on the train

236
00:16:57,141 --> 00:16:59,900
and pulls it down the guideway.

237
00:16:59,901 --> 00:17:03,180
It's like a surfer stands on a surfboard.

238
00:17:03,181 --> 00:17:05,660
If the wave goes faster, the surfer goes faster.

239
00:17:05,661 --> 00:17:08,340
If the wave slows down, the surfer slows down.

240
00:17:08,341 --> 00:17:09,940
We're doing the same thing with electronics.

241
00:17:12,700 --> 00:17:17,300
Perhaps most remarkably, there is no onboard operator.

242
00:17:17,301 --> 00:17:19,740
The TransRapid is essentially the world's largest

243
00:17:19,741 --> 00:17:22,833
radio controlled train. We have no driver.

244
00:17:22,834 --> 00:17:26,220
We have a control room and so the whole system

245
00:17:26,221 --> 00:17:28,900
is run using very sophisticated computers.

246
00:17:28,901 --> 00:17:32,490
We can see where the train is at all times.

247
00:17:32,491 --> 00:17:36,080
The control is speed from right here in the

248
00:17:36,081 --> 00:17:42,660
control room. All functions of the train can be monitored from right here.

249
00:17:42,661 --> 00:17:47,660
The control room uses radio signals to constantly monitor and manage the progress of the train.

250
00:17:47,661 --> 00:17:52,480
The guideway itself is controlled in the very same fashion.

251
00:17:54,560 --> 00:17:58,160
The motor in our train is actually in the guideway.

252
00:17:58,700 --> 00:18:02,960
And we power that motor from substations which are located along the route.

253
00:18:03,680 --> 00:18:07,000
And we feed that power out through cables up to the guideway.

254
00:18:07,640 --> 00:18:11,760
But we actually send it to the guideway at about half-mile increments.

255
00:18:12,520 --> 00:18:15,240
And we turn each half-mile on as the train goes by.

256
00:18:16,040 --> 00:18:17,840
And so as the train is moving down the track,

257
00:18:17,900 --> 00:18:21,420
we're actually switching the guideway on and off as the train moves down.

258
00:18:22,200 --> 00:18:26,580
But far from the test track in Germany, the Shanghai Maglev turned heads.

259
00:18:26,581 --> 00:18:28,820
And not only for its record-breaking speed,

260
00:18:29,360 --> 00:18:32,300
its massive construction was also remarkably rapid.

261
00:18:33,640 --> 00:18:36,460
The Shanghai project was phenomenal.

262
00:18:36,760 --> 00:18:38,580
The total project took less than three years,

263
00:18:38,820 --> 00:18:43,040
from signing of contracts to the opening day, which is a record in itself.

264
00:18:43,041 --> 00:18:47,380
Running 20 miles from the city's financial center to the airport,

265
00:18:47,760 --> 00:18:51,480
it has already proven the speed and reliability of maglev technology.

266
00:18:52,980 --> 00:18:56,260
The train runs on time and is rarely out of service.

267
00:18:57,420 --> 00:18:59,460
After about a year and a half of operation,

268
00:18:59,680 --> 00:19:01,900
the train is actually running better than we all thought.

269
00:19:02,440 --> 00:19:06,460
We're currently running at about a 99.6% uptime.

270
00:19:07,320 --> 00:19:10,500
120 more miles of guideway is already being planned

271
00:19:10,501 --> 00:19:12,580
to the seaport city of Hangzhou.

272
00:19:13,040 --> 00:19:21,040
This addition alone is nearly 10 times longer than the very first railroad tracks laid in the United States nearly two centuries ago.

273
00:19:22,040 --> 00:19:25,368
Rail travel really came into being in the mid-1820s.

274
00:19:25,369 --> 00:19:30,040
The Baltimore and Ohio Railroad became the nation's first common carrier.

275
00:19:30,041 --> 00:19:33,040
Their first segment of track was about 13 miles.

276
00:19:34,040 --> 00:19:40,040
Using primitive wood-burning engines, these early trains could attain speeds near 20 miles per hour.

277
00:19:40,041 --> 00:19:44,040
The American Civil War would provide the next great leap

278
00:19:44,041 --> 00:19:47,040
in train speed and technology.

279
00:19:47,041 --> 00:19:49,040
Speeds were increasing.

280
00:19:49,041 --> 00:19:52,040
You saw trains moving up to 40 miles an hour,

281
00:19:52,041 --> 00:19:56,040
and the technology had moved to a boiler that now

282
00:19:56,041 --> 00:20:00,040
was oriented horizontally, allowing the greater speeds.

283
00:20:00,041 --> 00:20:03,040
By the turn of the 20th century,

284
00:20:03,041 --> 00:20:06,040
steam engine technology had been supercharged.

285
00:20:06,041 --> 00:20:12,040
By burning a hotter fuel, coal, these engines could attain even greater velocities.

286
00:20:12,041 --> 00:20:19,040
They're trying to draw as much power out of the steel wheel on the steel rail as they

287
00:20:19,041 --> 00:20:24,040
possibly can, and there's some marvelous technology that's being developed during this period.

288
00:20:24,041 --> 00:20:29,040
One visual innovation of the first decades of the 20th century was the curb appeal of

289
00:20:29,041 --> 00:20:31,040
these newly streamlined engines.

290
00:20:31,041 --> 00:20:37,040
The Pennsylvania Railroad GG1 electric locomotive was developed in the 1930s,

291
00:20:37,041 --> 00:20:41,040
very capable of speeds over 100 miles an hour.

292
00:20:41,041 --> 00:20:48,040
This train was a beautiful maroon streak traveling across the Jersey and Pennsylvania countryside.

293
00:20:48,041 --> 00:20:52,040
The Second World War would mark the end of the railroad's golden age.

294
00:20:52,041 --> 00:20:56,040
For after the war, passenger traffic plummeted.

295
00:20:56,041 --> 00:20:57,003
plummeted.

296
00:20:57,004 --> 00:21:04,318
It was because automobiles and airlines offered freedom combined with speed.

297
00:21:04,319 --> 00:21:05,280
And in the

298
00:21:05,281 --> 00:21:13,140
50s and 60s, you saw some interesting last gasps of railroads attempting to push technology for

299
00:21:13,141 --> 00:21:16,404
speed. One of those is the General Motors aerotrain.

300
00:21:16,405 --> 00:21:19,480
Using diesel technology, General Motors developed

301
00:21:19,481 --> 00:21:22,538
a train that was capable of traveling 102 miles an hour.

302
00:21:22,539 --> 00:21:24,940
Unfortunately, there were some technological

303
00:21:24,941 --> 00:21:30,040
flaws with the train and it didn't take off and garner the curb appeal that they wanted.

304
00:21:31,220 --> 00:21:37,020
Beginning in the 1960s, some countries started to offer higher speeds by placing new drive

305
00:21:37,021 --> 00:21:39,227
systems on the standard rails.

306
00:21:39,228 --> 00:21:43,640
And some prime examples of this are things like the Japanese

307
00:21:43,641 --> 00:21:50,940
bullet trains, an electric powered train running steel wheel on steel rail, yet attaining speeds

308
00:21:50,941 --> 00:21:53,040
over 186 miles an hour.

309
00:21:54,940 --> 00:22:01,940
Another design, the French TGV, or train a grand vitesse,

310
00:22:01,941 --> 00:22:04,940
is a wholly integrated system with updated track

311
00:22:04,941 --> 00:22:06,940
and switching technology.

312
00:22:06,941 --> 00:22:11,940
It set the old rail speed record of 236 miles per hour,

313
00:22:11,941 --> 00:22:14,940
which stood until the Shanghai Maglev smashed it

314
00:22:14,941 --> 00:22:17,940
by over 30 miles an hour.

315
00:22:17,941 --> 00:22:20,940
And when will the United States get into the air

316
00:22:20,941 --> 00:22:26,940
And when will the United States get its chance to join the Maglev Club?

317
00:22:26,941 --> 00:22:31,940
In the US, actually, we're pursuing five or more projects at the moment.

318
00:22:31,941 --> 00:22:35,940
The furthest advance are in Pittsburgh and in Las Vegas.

319
00:22:35,941 --> 00:22:38,940
And the Las Vegas project eventually will go all the way to Los Angeles.

320
00:22:38,941 --> 00:22:44,940
We're currently waiting for the Congress to pass the next all-encompassing infrastructure law,

321
00:22:44,941 --> 00:22:47,940
and we're hoping that construction money will be in that law

322
00:22:47,941 --> 00:22:49,940
for the first Maglev project in the United States.

323
00:22:50,940 --> 00:22:55,260
While the speed record set in Shanghai may see challengers in the near future,

324
00:22:56,340 --> 00:23:01,940
another far more dangerous world's fastest mark has stood firm for nearly three decades.

325
00:23:03,260 --> 00:23:08,580
The magnetic field generated by the trans-rapid maglev exerts effects on its passengers

326
00:23:08,581 --> 00:23:13,360
that are only 10% as powerful as those received from using a typical hair dryer.

327
00:23:20,940 --> 00:23:27,940
This boat, the spirit of Australia, is ready to tackle the world's water speed record.

328
00:23:27,941 --> 00:23:32,940
Quite simply, seeking that record is a most lethal pastime.

329
00:23:32,941 --> 00:23:39,940
The world's water speed record is probably the most dangerous sport on Earth.

330
00:23:39,941 --> 00:23:46,940
Since World War II, it has an 85% death rate, so it's not something you take lightly.

331
00:23:50,940 --> 00:23:54,820
Ken Warby holds the record as the world's fastest man on water,

332
00:23:55,260 --> 00:23:59,320
with a speed of nearly 318 miles per hour.

333
00:24:00,600 --> 00:24:05,740
Even more remarkable is that this still unmatched performance took place in 1978.

334
00:24:08,120 --> 00:24:15,260
There's been nobody else in history that ever designed, built, driven, and paid for

335
00:24:15,261 --> 00:24:17,960
their own boat to break the world water speed record.

336
00:24:17,961 --> 00:24:22,960
Ken Warby's lifelong obsession began at an impressionable age.

337
00:24:22,961 --> 00:24:29,960
In the early teens, I saw a drawing of Donald Campbell's Bluebird and bought some balsa,

338
00:24:29,961 --> 00:24:34,960
bought a little Jet-X solid propellant rocket, and fired the boat a few times across the

339
00:24:34,961 --> 00:24:39,960
pond and went back and said to my mother, I said, Mom, one day I'm going to do this

340
00:24:39,961 --> 00:24:40,960
for real.

341
00:24:40,961 --> 00:24:44,960
Soon he was building and racing his own boats.

342
00:24:44,961 --> 00:24:51,040
Probably the most successful one was a little racing skiff called Monte Cristo.

343
00:24:51,760 --> 00:24:55,560
I actually took out three state records in Australia with it.

344
00:24:55,561 --> 00:25:01,960
I raced outboards, hydroplanes, twin cockpit runabouts, just anything that floated.

345
00:25:02,120 --> 00:25:04,080
And if I could put my backside in, I did.

346
00:25:04,740 --> 00:25:07,580
But Warby's dream was to build a jet-powered boat.

347
00:25:08,620 --> 00:25:10,780
He followed that dream on a tight budget.

348
00:25:10,781 --> 00:25:19,400
The Spirit of Australia was designed literally one Saturday night on the kitchen table, sat

349
00:25:19,401 --> 00:25:26,000
down with a 20 by 30 sheet of paper, a pencil, a ruler and drew what I figured would be the

350
00:25:26,001 --> 00:25:28,586
ideal hull to fit a jet engine on.

351
00:25:28,587 --> 00:25:32,540
This boat was literally built in the backyard, under

352
00:25:32,541 --> 00:25:39,220
a tree, no garage, no nothing, literally built out of plywood and spruce frame and covered

353
00:25:39,221 --> 00:25:44,220
in fiberglass and a little else, just a whole lot of work.

354
00:25:44,221 --> 00:25:47,600
All Warby needed was an engine.

355
00:25:47,601 --> 00:25:51,220
A friend of mine told me of some Westinghouse J34 engines.

356
00:25:51,221 --> 00:25:52,540
They'd just scrapped it.

357
00:25:52,541 --> 00:25:54,920
And there were very few people at the auction.

358
00:25:54,921 --> 00:25:56,300
Nobody wanted jet engines.

359
00:25:56,301 --> 00:26:00,660
And the sum total of the money I had in my pocket was $65.

360
00:26:00,661 --> 00:26:05,720
I put my hand up, bid $65, got the engine.

361
00:26:05,721 --> 00:26:07,900
Finally, it was time for Ken to begin

362
00:26:07,901 --> 00:26:09,400
his assault on the world record.

363
00:26:10,900 --> 00:26:12,620
Ken decided to do it incrementally.

364
00:26:13,060 --> 00:26:16,340
He went out and first wanted to see if he could go 200 miles an hour.

365
00:26:16,460 --> 00:26:18,820
And then he went out again and he went about 260.

366
00:26:18,940 --> 00:26:21,300
And he bumped it up in a number of runs.

367
00:26:21,460 --> 00:26:23,900
And that's the reason Ken's alive and none of the other guys are.

368
00:26:26,020 --> 00:26:31,700
Despite the danger, October of 1978 found Ken and the spirit of Australia

369
00:26:31,701 --> 00:26:34,900
ready to take the record at Flowering Dam near Sydney.

370
00:26:34,901 --> 00:26:40,780
There always is through the day a wind pattern that goes right down the center of that lake

371
00:26:40,781 --> 00:26:47,020
because what we're looking for is a chop, about a six-inch chop, and get on the top of it,

372
00:26:47,120 --> 00:26:49,520
and it creates little air pockets underneath the boat.

373
00:26:49,720 --> 00:26:51,680
You want to aerate under your boat and go quicker.

374
00:26:52,900 --> 00:26:57,000
Going quick in motorized boats has fascinated racers for over 100 years.

375
00:26:58,380 --> 00:27:03,700
Organized powerboat racing began in 1903 with a race called the Harmsworth Trophy Race.

376
00:27:03,701 --> 00:27:09,140
All of the contestants, the boat, the motor, everything had to represent a specific country.

377
00:27:09,880 --> 00:27:15,260
The first Harmsworth Cup was one with a top speed of merely 21.4 miles per hour.

378
00:27:18,940 --> 00:27:22,500
Soon, some very notable individuals were building fast boats,

379
00:27:23,020 --> 00:27:26,400
like Alexander Graham Bell and his futuristic-looking hydrofoil.

380
00:27:27,940 --> 00:27:33,160
Bell produced a hydrofoil that had airplane propellers on the back,

381
00:27:33,161 --> 00:27:37,840
and he went about 70 miles an hour in 1919.

382
00:27:38,200 --> 00:27:39,680
For a boat, that was incredible.

383
00:27:40,820 --> 00:27:43,680
Bell was denied the official world water record

384
00:27:43,681 --> 00:27:46,200
because of the unorthodox nature of his craft.

385
00:27:47,700 --> 00:27:52,880
By 1931, racers were quickly closing in on the 100-mile-an-hour mark.

386
00:27:53,760 --> 00:27:55,280
Two teams battled for the record,

387
00:27:55,680 --> 00:27:57,640
one British, led by Henry Seagrave,

388
00:27:57,740 --> 00:27:59,940
and one American, led by Gar Wood.

389
00:27:59,941 --> 00:28:04,420
Seagrave decided that he was going to get that record or die trying.

390
00:28:04,640 --> 00:28:07,780
So he took the Miss England 2 out and he hit something in the water.

391
00:28:07,900 --> 00:28:09,360
The boat turned over and he was killed.

392
00:28:10,700 --> 00:28:16,200
Just weeks later, Garwood became the first man to break 100 miles per hour on water.

393
00:28:18,140 --> 00:28:22,000
Around this time, a new hull design revolutionized boat racing.

394
00:28:23,260 --> 00:28:24,320
The hydroplane.

395
00:28:24,321 --> 00:28:32,180
A hydroplane is any boat that's designed to skim across the surface of the water rather than cut through the water.

396
00:28:33,020 --> 00:28:37,440
Hydroplanes use floats, or sponsons, to lift them up on top of the water.

397
00:28:38,200 --> 00:28:39,940
Soon they began to dominate racing.

398
00:28:41,620 --> 00:28:51,560
By 1950, the water speed record had risen to 180 miles an hour, courtesy of an American hydroplane, the ironically named slow motion.

399
00:28:52,560 --> 00:28:55,180
That speed caught the latest English team's leader,

400
00:28:55,660 --> 00:28:58,260
the now legendary Donald Campbell, by surprise.

401
00:29:00,260 --> 00:29:05,740
Campbell knew the only way to go faster was with a new power source, a jet engine.

402
00:29:06,160 --> 00:29:09,820
So he had one of the world's first jet boats built to his specifications.

403
00:29:10,540 --> 00:29:12,540
He named it Bluebird K7.

404
00:29:13,440 --> 00:29:18,820
It was a catamaran three-point, so it had a center body and then two pontoons on each side.

405
00:29:18,821 --> 00:29:20,960
The boat was very, very fast.

406
00:29:21,560 --> 00:29:26,920
In 1955, Campbell smashed the 200-mile-an-hour mark in the jet-powered Bluebird.

407
00:29:28,360 --> 00:29:35,600
Over the next decade, he bested his own mark six times, ending with a record of 276 miles an hour.

408
00:29:37,040 --> 00:29:39,400
In England, he was a national icon.

409
00:29:40,320 --> 00:29:42,460
But Campbell still wasn't satisfied.

410
00:29:43,380 --> 00:29:46,300
He wanted to be the first man to go over 300 miles an hour.

411
00:29:47,000 --> 00:29:51,520
And by 1967, the Bluebird K7 was a pretty tired machine.

412
00:29:52,220 --> 00:29:58,060
Nevertheless, Campbell left London for Lake Coniston in the north of England to break 300 miles per hour.

413
00:29:58,600 --> 00:30:01,240
His first early morning run was blazingly fast.

414
00:30:01,880 --> 00:30:10,000
He had planned to stop, fill the boat with fuel, let the wake dissipate from his up-lake run, and then run back.

415
00:30:10,120 --> 00:30:15,060
And for reasons that no one quite understands, he didn't refuel, and he ran back down the lake,

416
00:30:15,480 --> 00:30:20,920
with the boat much lighter than it normally is because it was low on fuel, and ran right over his own wake.

417
00:30:20,921 --> 00:30:24,060
The boat left the surface of the water, tumbled end for end,

418
00:30:24,160 --> 00:30:26,220
and crashed into the water in a horrific accident.

419
00:30:28,880 --> 00:30:33,300
He and his boat were on the bottom of Coniston for almost 35 years.

420
00:30:33,740 --> 00:30:36,940
But this tragedy didn't diminish Donald Campbell's legacy,

421
00:30:37,720 --> 00:30:41,020
which reached around the world to inspire a young boy in Australia.

422
00:30:42,140 --> 00:30:45,900
The record breakers, be it land or air or sea, whatever,

423
00:30:46,240 --> 00:30:47,620
they were the heroes of the day.

424
00:30:48,140 --> 00:30:50,680
And to me, Donald Campbell was a hero.

425
00:30:50,920 --> 00:30:54,920
But now it was Ken's turn to bask in glory.

426
00:30:54,921 --> 00:30:59,920
His speed of 317.596 miles per hour

427
00:30:59,921 --> 00:31:02,920
set a new world water speed record.

428
00:31:02,921 --> 00:31:05,920
Today, he remains the only man ever

429
00:31:05,921 --> 00:31:09,920
to go 300 miles per hour on water.

430
00:31:09,921 --> 00:31:12,920
While few of us will ever get to risk our lives

431
00:31:12,921 --> 00:31:15,920
piloting a jet boat, we all can experience

432
00:31:15,921 --> 00:31:19,920
a pounding heart and dry throat as this next world's fastest

433
00:31:19,921 --> 00:31:25,800
world's fastest machine, flings us into the air at 128 miles per hour.

434
00:31:28,320 --> 00:31:35,340
At his record-breaking speed of 317.5 miles per hour, Ken Warby could traverse the length

435
00:31:35,341 --> 00:31:38,240
of Lake Superior in just over one hour.

436
00:31:38,241 --> 00:31:51,480
Military-grade technology slams you with 5G force as you ascend skyward.

437
00:31:51,960 --> 00:31:55,960
Then weightlessness teases before you plummet straight back down to Earth.

438
00:31:57,220 --> 00:32:04,840
Meet the world's fastest thrill ride, the roller coaster called Kingda Ka, at Six Flags Great Adventure in Jackson, New Jersey.

439
00:32:04,841 --> 00:32:11,640
Nowhere else on this planet are you going to get a ride that launches you from 0 to 128

440
00:32:11,641 --> 00:32:14,500
miles per hour in 3.5 seconds.

441
00:32:14,501 --> 00:32:20,640
Kingda Ka's radical design was originally conceived in the digital realm.

442
00:32:20,641 --> 00:32:26,480
Ride design is so advanced now that once we've determined the style of ride that we want and

443
00:32:26,481 --> 00:32:30,620
we determine the area where we want to put this ride, we can generate everything within

444
00:32:30,621 --> 00:32:33,500
the computer.

445
00:32:33,501 --> 00:32:36,700
the huge ride proved as challenging as its design was daring.

446
00:32:39,280 --> 00:32:42,140
Weather plays such an important role in doing construction,

447
00:32:42,480 --> 00:32:43,560
particularly in the winter.

448
00:32:44,120 --> 00:32:49,220
One that may have been somewhat unforeseen in building this ride is the wind.

449
00:32:49,221 --> 00:32:52,680
You have two 500-foot cranes that are putting this ride up.

450
00:32:53,620 --> 00:32:58,200
The wind at 500 feet is obviously much higher than the wind on the ground.

451
00:32:59,100 --> 00:33:02,000
So on days when it might not even seem that windy,

452
00:33:02,001 --> 00:33:07,120
you can't actually fly steel because the wind is too high and the cranes cannot operate.

453
00:33:08,900 --> 00:33:13,600
One of Kingda Ka's most remarkable features is its breathtaking acceleration.

454
00:33:16,300 --> 00:33:19,860
The technology was borrowed from the launch deck of an aircraft carrier.

455
00:33:21,940 --> 00:33:24,960
Here we are in the hydraulic building, the heart of Kingda Ka.

456
00:33:25,440 --> 00:33:29,280
This is where we drive the coaster at 128 miles per hour.

457
00:33:29,281 --> 00:33:33,460
The driving force behind Kingda Ka is hydraulic fluid.

458
00:33:33,920 --> 00:33:38,660
The launching system is done with eight 500-horsepower motors

459
00:33:38,661 --> 00:33:42,860
that charge giant accumulators with hydraulic fluid.

460
00:33:43,340 --> 00:33:48,480
This fluid is pressurized much the same way that you might blow up a balloon.

461
00:33:49,100 --> 00:33:52,780
When the launch occurs, it's the same as letting that balloon go.

462
00:33:52,880 --> 00:33:53,960
That energy is released.

463
00:33:53,961 --> 00:34:02,740
That immense energy powers hydraulic motors that spin a huge winch, around which a cable

464
00:34:02,741 --> 00:34:03,441
runs.

465
00:34:03,442 --> 00:34:08,820
Attached to the end of the cable is a catch car that actually hurls the ride down

466
00:34:08,821 --> 00:34:09,577
the track.

467
00:34:09,578 --> 00:34:15,480
Once the catch car gets up to speed, it releases and the ride continues on its

468
00:34:15,481 --> 00:34:16,181
own.

469
00:34:17,160 --> 00:34:23,460
It takes just 10 seconds from the time the train is launched to when you are 456 feet

470
00:34:23,461 --> 00:34:26,100
on top of that tower.

471
00:34:26,101 --> 00:34:28,500
Hurtling straight down the huge tower,

472
00:34:28,501 --> 00:34:33,360
the ride again rockets to over 100 miles per hour.

473
00:34:33,361 --> 00:34:36,100
Riders actually experience another period of weightlessness

474
00:34:36,101 --> 00:34:39,340
as it crests another hill.

475
00:34:39,341 --> 00:34:42,860
Then it's time to bring this blazing meteor to a stop.

476
00:34:42,861 --> 00:34:47,900
For that, Kingda Ka employs a unique braking system.

477
00:34:47,901 --> 00:34:50,180
The braking system on Kingda Ka is done mainly

478
00:34:50,181 --> 00:34:51,460
with eddy current brakes.

479
00:34:53,460 --> 00:34:56,860
and zero contact.

480
00:34:57,360 --> 00:34:59,260
So there is no friction that occurs

481
00:34:59,261 --> 00:35:01,740
between the brake fin and the brake itself.

482
00:35:01,840 --> 00:35:03,500
It's all done through magnetic fields.

483
00:35:03,760 --> 00:35:04,700
So you hit the brakes,

484
00:35:04,760 --> 00:35:08,160
and you're still probably screaming, catching your breath,

485
00:35:08,161 --> 00:35:10,500
and the train kind of coasts back into the station.

486
00:35:12,260 --> 00:35:15,060
Roller coasters have always tested our nerves

487
00:35:15,061 --> 00:35:17,160
and our stomachs.

488
00:35:17,660 --> 00:35:19,440
But Kingda Ka's ancestors

489
00:35:19,441 --> 00:35:21,620
can barely be called thrill rides anymore.

490
00:35:23,460 --> 00:35:29,460
The first roller coaster in the United States was built in Coney Island, New York in 1884.

491
00:35:30,340 --> 00:35:33,220
It ran approximately eight miles per hour.

492
00:35:33,221 --> 00:35:36,580
The passengers would have to disembark the train,

493
00:35:36,581 --> 00:35:41,140
push it up to another deck so that it could make the return trip back.

494
00:35:43,140 --> 00:35:46,420
Changes came fast after the success of that first thriller.

495
00:35:47,940 --> 00:35:51,780
They decided that it was impractical for passengers to push the train,

496
00:35:51,781 --> 00:35:59,220
So they found a simple cable lift system that developed into the chain lift systems that we're familiar with today.

497
00:36:00,620 --> 00:36:05,540
By the roaring 20s, roller coasters could be found in almost every state in the Union.

498
00:36:06,600 --> 00:36:12,640
Some of the most famous coasters ever ridden came from this era, including the Cyclone at Coney Island.

499
00:36:13,140 --> 00:36:18,260
The Coney Island Cyclone is the most famous amusement ride in history.

500
00:36:18,261 --> 00:36:23,760
it still delivers a punch just as severe as any modern roller coaster.

501
00:36:24,700 --> 00:36:27,980
The giants of this era were still made of latticed wood,

502
00:36:28,440 --> 00:36:30,040
just like their earliest predecessors.

503
00:36:30,900 --> 00:36:32,900
But that, too, would eventually change.

504
00:36:34,680 --> 00:36:38,300
It wasn't until theme parks came along in the late 50s

505
00:36:38,301 --> 00:36:42,480
they decided, OK, let's try steel as a material.

506
00:36:42,960 --> 00:36:45,420
The first all-steel coaster took shape

507
00:36:45,421 --> 00:36:47,640
at the first modern-day American theme park.

508
00:36:48,260 --> 00:36:55,100
The first metal roller coaster as we know them that actually worked and actually still

509
00:36:55,101 --> 00:36:57,400
runs is the Matterhorn at Disneyland.

510
00:36:58,820 --> 00:37:03,000
Suddenly, by using steel, there were all kinds of new possibilities.

511
00:37:03,780 --> 00:37:06,340
They realized we can go upside down with this.

512
00:37:06,660 --> 00:37:08,400
We can get faster.

513
00:37:08,520 --> 00:37:09,380
We can get bigger.

514
00:37:09,800 --> 00:37:11,340
And that's exactly what they did.

515
00:37:11,960 --> 00:37:16,800
Since then, there have been no holds barred in the design and velocity of new so-called

516
00:37:16,801 --> 00:37:17,640
super coasters.

517
00:37:18,260 --> 00:37:21,260
which have carried riders to new, unprecedented thrills.

518
00:37:25,260 --> 00:37:27,800
But they all pale next to the new speed champ,

519
00:37:27,900 --> 00:37:33,500
the 128-mile-an-hour beast they call Kingda Ka.

520
00:37:33,600 --> 00:37:34,880
I love this ride.

521
00:37:34,980 --> 00:37:37,580
Sometimes at work, I just take a little break, go out,

522
00:37:37,680 --> 00:37:40,080
jump on the ride, and back to work.

523
00:37:40,180 --> 00:37:42,380
It really is a religious experience.

524
00:37:42,480 --> 00:37:45,580
But if Kingda Ka still isn't quick enough for your taste,

525
00:37:45,581 --> 00:37:49,180
there's a record holder much, much faster still.

526
00:37:49,280 --> 00:37:52,180
Prepare yourself for the fastest ride on Earth.

527
00:37:55,420 --> 00:37:58,820
The typical thrust needed for a successful launch of Kingda Ka

528
00:37:58,920 --> 00:38:01,020
is nearly 21,000 horsepower.

529
00:38:08,140 --> 00:38:12,340
Don't blink, or you might miss the fastest machine on Earth.

530
00:38:12,341 --> 00:38:16,940
3, 2, 1, 0, fire.

531
00:38:17,540 --> 00:38:22,640
We currently hold the world land speed record of 6,453 miles an hour.

532
00:38:22,641 --> 00:38:27,880
That is approximately Mach 8.6, or over 8 and a half times the speed of sound.

533
00:38:28,700 --> 00:38:33,760
That run was the fastest of nearly 11,000 tests that have been performed here on the

534
00:38:33,761 --> 00:38:35,880
Holloman High Speed Test Track in New Mexico.

535
00:38:35,881 --> 00:38:44,380
All of those scientific tests could only happen in flight high above the Earth, or in the

536
00:38:44,381 --> 00:38:48,300
more controlled simulated environment offered on the track.

537
00:38:48,301 --> 00:38:53,220
Those tests are on sleds, powered by a combination of rocket engines.

538
00:38:53,221 --> 00:38:58,640
Since each of those tests has its own unique requirements, every sled has to be custom-made.

539
00:38:58,641 --> 00:39:04,040
We have engineers and fabricators here on site so that we can design and build our sleds

540
00:39:04,041 --> 00:39:05,640
right here at Holloman.

541
00:39:05,641 --> 00:39:10,180
The sleds all run on unique skid-like devices called slippers.

542
00:39:10,820 --> 00:39:12,880
We don't use wheels on our rail.

543
00:39:12,980 --> 00:39:16,440
We actually use a slipper which wraps around the rail.

544
00:39:17,360 --> 00:39:21,900
If you had a wheeled vehicle, there'd be nothing to keep it from lifting off and flying into the air.

545
00:39:22,120 --> 00:39:26,700
They're going at speeds that are five, six, seven, eight times the speed of sound.

546
00:39:26,701 --> 00:39:28,300
You have to hold them onto the track.

547
00:39:30,080 --> 00:39:35,580
The sled that captured the land speed record was the product of more than 50 years of high-speed research.

548
00:39:35,640 --> 00:39:41,200
One of the track's first applications was testing missile launches.

549
00:39:42,280 --> 00:39:46,740
The test track here in Holloman actually had its first launch in 1950.

550
00:39:47,420 --> 00:39:51,360
The original track was only 3,500 feet long.

551
00:39:51,880 --> 00:39:57,200
And then in 1954, it was extended to support the testing of Dr. Stapp.

552
00:39:57,660 --> 00:40:02,900
Colonel John Stapp came to Holloman to determine the limits of human endurance to G-forces

553
00:40:02,901 --> 00:40:06,440
and became the fastest man on Earth in 1954.

554
00:40:07,120 --> 00:40:10,900
He was actually decelerated at minus 40 Gs,

555
00:40:11,200 --> 00:40:15,580
and he proved that if the human body properly restrained with seat belts

556
00:40:15,581 --> 00:40:18,320
could actually survive a very punishing deceleration.

557
00:40:19,800 --> 00:40:23,460
That deceleration caused numerous stress-related injuries,

558
00:40:23,940 --> 00:40:25,420
including temporary blindness.

559
00:40:26,680 --> 00:40:31,040
This was one reason the Air Force halted human testing at the end of 1954.

560
00:40:32,900 --> 00:40:37,800
But in the ensuing decades, a huge range of new protocols would be tested here.

561
00:40:39,220 --> 00:40:45,460
In the 1950s, we either were using launch assist to launch missiles off the track,

562
00:40:45,700 --> 00:40:49,400
or we were testing ejection seats for different aircraft.

563
00:40:49,680 --> 00:40:55,020
In the 60s, we started a lot of testing for ballistic missile guidance systems.

564
00:40:55,700 --> 00:41:01,820
In the 80s, we started doing quite a bit of work for the Missile Defense Agency to test different warheads.

565
00:41:01,821 --> 00:41:05,960
And then in the 90s, we started testing bunker buster type

566
00:41:05,961 --> 00:41:08,960
munitions, weapons that are supposed to penetrate into deep

567
00:41:08,961 --> 00:41:12,640
concrete bunkers and destroy high value targets like deep

568
00:41:12,641 --> 00:41:14,600
command and control bunkers.

569
00:41:14,601 --> 00:41:17,840
As the types of projects changed through the years,

570
00:41:17,841 --> 00:41:21,500
the track itself evolved to keep pace.

571
00:41:21,501 --> 00:41:25,640
Today, there are actually two tracks, one wide gauge

572
00:41:25,641 --> 00:41:27,480
and one narrow gauge.

573
00:41:27,481 --> 00:41:30,440
The original wide gauge track is used in slower tests,

574
00:41:31,380 --> 00:41:33,760
like those for aircraft ejection seats.

575
00:41:35,240 --> 00:41:39,120
The narrow gauge sled is used for hypersonic testing.

576
00:41:39,840 --> 00:41:42,540
The narrow gauge sled provides less air drag,

577
00:41:42,800 --> 00:41:45,180
and it's easier to accelerate a payload to higher velocity.

578
00:41:45,960 --> 00:41:48,660
Both sets of tracks share an alignment standard

579
00:41:48,661 --> 00:41:50,320
that has no equal in the world.

580
00:41:51,140 --> 00:41:54,200
It's aligned to about 25 thousandths of an inch

581
00:41:54,201 --> 00:41:55,300
along its entire length.

582
00:41:55,301 --> 00:42:00,140
We have a full-time rail survey crew that maintains tight tolerances.

583
00:42:01,340 --> 00:42:05,620
Which is essential for keeping sleds traveling at Mach 8 under control.

584
00:42:06,340 --> 00:42:10,400
But no living creature could survive a ride on this modern-day speed demon.

585
00:42:11,720 --> 00:42:16,220
We may pull up to 150 times the force of gravity.

586
00:42:16,820 --> 00:42:22,260
So if you weighed 200 pounds, it'd be like 30,000 pounds of force on your body,

587
00:42:22,320 --> 00:42:23,720
and no one would survive that.

588
00:42:23,721 --> 00:42:28,720
Such high speeds also generate huge amounts of friction.

589
00:42:28,721 --> 00:42:35,720
Scientists at the track have developed a unique method for combating friction's number one byproduct, heat.

590
00:42:35,721 --> 00:42:42,720
We actually take a piece of plastic thousands of feet long and then we inflate it full of helium.

591
00:42:42,721 --> 00:42:49,720
That helium reduces both the drag and any of the heating effects you get from going through the air very fast.

592
00:42:49,721 --> 00:42:52,560
This saves the sled from potentially burning up

593
00:42:52,660 --> 00:42:57,200
in ultra-high speed runs, like the land speed record attempt.

594
00:42:57,300 --> 00:43:02,200
That run was the culmination of a four and a half year project.

595
00:43:02,300 --> 00:43:05,200
We began to try to set a new world record

596
00:43:05,300 --> 00:43:07,440
to support the Missile Defense Agency,

597
00:43:07,540 --> 00:43:11,700
because their impacts were going to go from about a Mach 6 impact

598
00:43:11,800 --> 00:43:13,940
up to about a Mach 9 impact.

599
00:43:14,040 --> 00:43:17,120
The world record sled was actually four stages.

600
00:43:17,121 --> 00:43:20,120
The first stage was five rocket motors.

601
00:43:20,121 --> 00:43:23,120
They produced about 200,000 pounds of thrust.

602
00:43:23,121 --> 00:43:25,120
Then our second stage took over.

603
00:43:25,121 --> 00:43:30,120
Six more motors, about 250,000 pounds of thrust.

604
00:43:30,121 --> 00:43:34,120
The last two stages, using powerful new super roadrunner engines,

605
00:43:34,121 --> 00:43:38,120
each putting out 228,000 pounds of thrust,

606
00:43:38,121 --> 00:43:40,120
really got the sled moving.

607
00:43:40,121 --> 00:43:42,120
At that point in time, the sled's accelerating

608
00:43:42,121 --> 00:43:45,120
about 150 times the force of gravity,

609
00:43:45,121 --> 00:43:47,160
of gravity, and we reach our peak velocity

610
00:43:47,260 --> 00:43:49,860
at 6,453 miles an hour.

611
00:43:49,960 --> 00:43:51,300
Which only confirmed what they've

612
00:43:51,400 --> 00:43:54,500
been seeing at Holloman for a long time.

613
00:43:54,600 --> 00:43:56,240
The Holloman high-speed test track

614
00:43:56,340 --> 00:43:57,900
is the fastest ride on Earth.

